WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.634 --> 00:00:02.870
(instrumental music)

00:00:02.870 --> 00:00:04.440
- [Narrator] Welcome to Iowa,

00:00:04.440 --> 00:00:06.343
where wind turbines rule the land.

00:00:08.700 --> 00:00:10.230
And in the town of Esthervile,

00:00:10.230 --> 00:00:11.780
there is a wind program dishing out

00:00:11.780 --> 00:00:14.600
some of the best wind
technicians in the country.

00:00:14.600 --> 00:00:15.550
- I'm Joel Caulfield,

00:00:15.550 --> 00:00:17.240
and I'm here with my dad, Jim Caulfield.

00:00:17.240 --> 00:00:19.320
- We here are students at
Iowa Lakes Community College,

00:00:19.320 --> 00:00:21.844
and we're studying to be
wind turbine technicians.

00:00:21.844 --> 00:00:24.927
(instrumental music)

00:00:27.655 --> 00:00:30.155
(light music)

00:00:31.400 --> 00:00:33.710
- [Narrator] This is Iowa
Lakes Community College-

00:00:33.710 --> 00:00:36.070
a school that offers an
intensive two-year program

00:00:36.070 --> 00:00:38.672
in applied science and
wind turbine technology.

00:00:38.672 --> 00:00:41.270
Jim and his son, Joel,
are students who entered

00:00:41.270 --> 00:00:42.323
the program together.

00:00:44.240 --> 00:00:46.470
- A wind technician is
somebody who would actually

00:00:46.470 --> 00:00:48.160
work on the wind turbine to make sure

00:00:48.160 --> 00:00:49.910
that it is being serviced properly

00:00:49.910 --> 00:00:53.370
and, if it breaks down,
actually be able to repair it.

00:00:53.370 --> 00:00:55.300
- We just basically take a variations

00:00:55.300 --> 00:00:57.800
of different electrical theory classes,

00:00:57.800 --> 00:00:59.590
all the way up to advanced level stuff

00:00:59.590 --> 00:01:01.280
of actually wiring up circuits

00:01:01.280 --> 00:01:03.633
and advanced programming of computers.

00:01:05.270 --> 00:01:07.910
- Wind turbine is actually
big pieces of equipment

00:01:07.910 --> 00:01:10.810
that carry a lot of risk, and
that's why they are ingraining

00:01:10.810 --> 00:01:12.670
in us everything we need to know,

00:01:12.670 --> 00:01:13.890
so that when we're up there we know how

00:01:13.890 --> 00:01:16.040
to handle every situation
we can deal with.

00:01:20.200 --> 00:01:21.290
- When you're going out to a turbine

00:01:21.290 --> 00:01:23.270
for the first time, you're
very anxious and nervous

00:01:23.270 --> 00:01:24.840
because you're going at a height

00:01:24.840 --> 00:01:26.993
that you're never been to before.

00:01:29.300 --> 00:01:32.253
I mean there's a lot of fall
potential that could happen.

00:01:34.330 --> 00:01:35.980
- To elaborate on what Joel's saying,

00:01:35.980 --> 00:01:39.763
it's serious stuff that you
don't take lightly at all.

00:01:41.210 --> 00:01:43.530
- When you get to the top,
you just start to get more

00:01:43.530 --> 00:01:46.140
of an excitement over fear and exhaustion

00:01:46.140 --> 00:01:49.010
because now you're up
there in the cell itself,

00:01:49.010 --> 00:01:50.743
so it's actually kind of cool to see

00:01:50.743 --> 00:01:53.173
a working turbine in first action.

00:01:58.490 --> 00:02:00.280
- [Narrator] For Jim and
Joel, going back to school

00:02:00.280 --> 00:02:01.660
was a no-brainer.

00:02:01.660 --> 00:02:04.180
Jim left the trucking
industry after 26 years

00:02:04.180 --> 00:02:06.710
to follow his passion in renewable energy,

00:02:06.710 --> 00:02:08.560
and his son, Joel, wanted
to travel the world

00:02:08.560 --> 00:02:11.530
and work with this hands, so
enrolling in the wind program

00:02:11.530 --> 00:02:13.410
at the same time, while unconventional,

00:02:13.410 --> 00:02:15.490
ended up working out perfectly.

00:02:15.490 --> 00:02:17.660
- We can bounce off each other for ideas

00:02:17.660 --> 00:02:19.800
and stuff like that, help
each other with like homework,

00:02:19.800 --> 00:02:22.893
so it kind of gave us an
opportunity to grow better.

00:02:24.441 --> 00:02:26.930
- There's a lot of things
that I'm still scratchin'

00:02:26.930 --> 00:02:29.660
my head on, and, you know, he'll
just come by, and he's like

00:02:29.660 --> 00:02:31.380
well, you gotta do this, you gotta,

00:02:31.380 --> 00:02:33.213
how the heck do you know that?

00:02:33.213 --> 00:02:35.580
How the heck you know,
but he does, he gets it.

00:02:35.580 --> 00:02:37.660
It's actually a great feeling knowing that

00:02:37.660 --> 00:02:41.060
I have a chance to, you know,
pursue a career in something

00:02:41.060 --> 00:02:43.695
that I've been very passionate about.

00:02:43.695 --> 00:02:46.240
There are other people doing this,

00:02:46.240 --> 00:02:48.810
and it's worth it for anybody to go ahead

00:02:48.810 --> 00:02:50.877
and make that next leap.

00:02:50.877 --> 00:02:53.377
(light music)

